Phase 6: Amortize atmospheric and repeated density/phase evaluation
  Depends on Phase 5.1. Outcome: shading avoids repeated work with explicitly qualified approximations.
  ☐ Move aerial/local-fog application out of every density-shading sample toward representative-
    distance composition; derive the representative distance and apply the existing transport once.
    - Preserve physical cloud history, underwater/local-medium selection, coverage, lighting range
      admission and scene encoding. Decide the owning pass and invalidate history when required.
    - Preserve distributed OIT bucket composition: derive whether one distance is adequate or each
      bucket needs a representative distance. Do not flatten the volume to a depthless overlay.
    - Compare to existing per-sample transport for deep clouds, foreground/translucent overlap,
      horizon, underwater and changing fog; choose bounded extra distance evaluations if needed.
    - Test the specified representative-distance formula, OIT bookkeeping and once-only encoding
      independently. Differences from per-sample transport are diagnostics for measured-cost and
      user appearance review, not automated approximation-error or fidelity acceptance gates.
  ☐ Hoist fixed directional phase evaluation to once per primary ray/light and reuse it for every
    occupied sample. Check generated shader behavior so compiler hoisting is not claimed as new savings.
  ☐ Reduce repeated cloud_volume_density.glsl work; equivalent optimizations preserve the density field.
    - Reuse neighboring column descriptors within a sampled interval; retain height-dependent
      occupancy tests and native validity rules. Avoid neighbor lookups when they cannot affect erosion.
    - Skip noise when amplitude is zero and skip detailed work when cheap support proves zero density.
      Retain shared-face continuity, cubic bounds and world/wind anchoring for view and light rays.
    - Measure before changing procedural noise to a reusable noise texture; such a texture supplies
      detail only, not a complete density volume. A texture reproducing the current field requires
      same-field equivalence checks. An intentional noise-field replacement is a separate approximation
      choice: record statistical/output differences diagnostically and obtain user appearance review,
      without automated spectral-quality or fidelity thresholds. Both choices retain native support,
      connected faces, anchoring and the shared view/light density definition. Retain current noise
      if no measured benefit justifies replacement.
  ☐ Completion: specified atmospheric composition/OIT arithmetic passes correctness checks;
    density-preserving optimizations and phase hoisting pass same-algorithm equivalence checks.
    Intentional atmosphere/noise approximation changes have diagnostic comparisons and the stated
    review evidence rather than old-output equivalence gates. Attributed savings come from measurements.

Phase 7: Make acceleration optional and efficient for the new integration schedule
  Depends on Phase 6. Outcome: empty-space rejection helps without repeated dense-path overhead.
  ☐ Compare plain column traversal, cheap root rejection and the current hierarchy under identical
    new sample budgets; include construction and GPU barriers in total cost.
    - Consider cheap conservative density rejection and coarse start-distance rejection without
      assuming another prepass or a particular tree traversal is required.
  ☐ For retained hierarchical traversal, carry node/interval progress through the ray and reuse
    valid ancestor bounds instead of restarting root descent after every surviving column.
    - Traverse coherent node intervals front-to-back, ascend only when leaving their footprint and
      advance across missed regions without replaying every skipped native grid boundary twice.
    - Preserve conservative geometry, boundary ties, parallel axes, padding, thin positive density
      and forward progress. Define numerical tolerances independently of old repeated-addition clocks.
      Revisit the recorded baseline one-event secondary-counter discrepancies when validating
      numerical progress; isolate instrumentation effects from actual missed or duplicated support.
    - Preserve the new sample schedule across acceleration choices; skipped empty nodes must not
      change quadrature, primary jitter or secondary midpoint positions. Retain bounded direct-march
      fallback on the same lattice when summaries are unavailable; do not restore per-column budgets.
    - Keep certificates and generation rebuild rules for any retained summaries; never sample stale
      data. Stop allocating/building summaries when the selected policy has no active consumer.
  ☐ Select the production acceleration policy from matched measurements. Retain cheap rejection or
    direct marching where a tree does not pay for itself; do not require hierarchy use merely because
    it exists. Any DDA retained here locates support for the same scheduled points, not integration cells.
    - Avoid an unmeasured per-frame heuristic, CPU texture readback or settings polling. Record
      selection inputs, transitions, resource demand and any remaining workload regressions.
    - If the tree is retired, remove its unused production resources/program registration and update
      current architecture/catalog contracts; retain only useful independent validation or benchmarks.
  ☐ Completion: acceleration preserves the same scheduled evaluations and transport results, with
    tolerances limited to floating-point evaluation differences in equivalent implementations.
    These are correctness checks, not acceptable approximation-error limits; sample placement,
    conservative support and clipping contracts remain unchanged. Total costs support the selected
    policy and dense/inside-cloud regressions are resolved or open.

