      source invalidation, current settings/defaults, reference selection and failed-resource recovery.
    - Keep density/bounds oracles independent; a new integrator must not validate only against itself.
  ☐ Measure baseline versus final renderer with matched scenes/settings and separate quality labels.
    - Report each increment's effects where attributable; distinguish fewer pixels/samples and
      changed approximation quality from same-output traversal/implementation improvements.
    - Include empty/sparse/dense clouds; ground/inside/above views; thin edges, low-angle solar/lunar,
      moving weather/light, odd maps and foreground/translucent/liquid overlap in both PBR modes.
    - Include the prior 512x512 regression matrix and representative full-view nonempty resolutions.
      Separate adversarial synthetic cases from native captures and live game evidence.
    - Record construction, trace, temporal, resolve, independently timed total, CPU submission,
      nominal storage, measured resident memory when available, hardware, hashes and raw samples.
      Account for native producer/uploads and shared depth separately in whole-frame assessment.
    - Report paired distributions and all regressions. Relative improvement is required; do not
      invent an absolute time target, hide regressions in an aggregate or equate probe counts with speed.
  ☐ Obtain user-run acceptance of the changed quality policy: cubic fluff, lighting, shadows within
    clouds, fog, thin support, disocclusion, motion and OIT overlap across relevant views/modes.
    Earlier appearance acceptance does not establish acceptance of changed sampling/resolution/fog.
  ☐ Update PBR.Clouds.md to describe only delivered behavior. Reconcile both source proposals,
    existing cloud checklists and the parent with implemented policy, remaining dependencies and
    receipts. Retire superseded requirements explicitly without rewriting prior measurement evidence.
  ☐ Completion: required correctness/package checks, second review, independent audit, measured
    relative benefit and user acceptance exist; unresolved performance or appearance gates stay open.

Final acceptance
  ☐ Whole-ray primary/shadow budgets, finite secondary distance and qualified threshold
    termination replace the identified unbounded-by-quality per-column work multiplication.
  ☐ Lower-resolution tracing, amortized atmosphere, repeated density/phase work and acceleration
    each have delivered behavior or an evidence-backed disposition against the stated objective.
  ☐ All six performance-analysis findings and the earlier density/phase findings map to final receipts;
    any unimplemented improvement is explicitly recorded, not silently treated as complete.
  ☐ Native ownership/weather, cubic density support, physical lighting, foreground clips, temporal
    lifecycle, existing OIT integration and both PBR modes satisfy inherited unaffected contracts.
  ☐ The parent remains unchecked while required live/performance gates or its separate reflected-
    cloud integration dependency remain outstanding. No headless result establishes live frame time.

Bounded quality policy (implementation contract)
  This section specifies the complete target behavior. Primary controls are implemented separately
    from later secondary, termination and reconstruction changes. Candidate defaults below are fixed
    starting inputs for comparisons, not a claim of validated appearance or a shipped default.

  Work accounting and units
    - Runtime retains one metre per world unit and 50-world-unit native columns. Extinction is
      map.r/(50*metresPerWorldUnit); path lengths enter transport in metres exactly once.
    - Geometry queries and OIT bookkeeping are separate from full density/source-lighting evaluations.
      Count both categories; a bounded shading budget does not imply constant acceleration overhead.
    - Report actual primary rays, secondary rays, primary/secondary density evaluations, native leaf
      fetches, hierarchy queries and skipped nodes separately where measured. An upper bound or CPU
      oracle estimate must not be presented as an observed GPU counter.

  Primary budget
    - Intersect the finite horizontal map domain and current main-view opaque/primary/liquid clips
      to obtain [tStart,tEnd]. A zero-length span performs no march. Let L=tEnd-tStart, including
      empty gaps. Use that same span across acceleration choices. Certified height bounds may reject
      an empty ray or skip proven-empty lattice positions; they do not redefine the sample count,
      spacing or origin. Do not add a support prepass to calculate occupied length or optical mass.
    - Set Nview=min(ViewRaySampleMax,max(ViewRaySampleMin,
      floor(ViewRaySampleMax*min(1,L/ViewBudgetDistance)))). Set ds=L/Nview. Sample positions are
      tStart+(i+jitter)*ds for i=0..Nview-1, with one blue-noise jitter in [0,1) shared along this ray.
      The history-free deterministic reference uses jitter=.5 on the same uniform distance lattice.
    - Current primary controls are ViewRaySampleMax=384, ViewRaySampleMin=2 and
      ViewBudgetDistance=50 world units. Earlier tuning receipts remain historical; retired
      approximation thresholds do not justify increasing these controls. Final cost and
      appearance selection remains separate measured/manual work.
      Supported maxima are 4..384; minimum is 1..min(16,max); distance is 50..10000 world units.
      Nonfinite distance restores 50; sanitize integral controls before conversion/upload.
    - At each position evaluate cheap native validity/support first, then the existing detailed
      density if potentially occupied. Positive contributing density evaluates incident lighting
      at that same position and starts at most one shadow ray per nonzero solar/lunar source.
      Do not reuse a detail factor or source from another interval, or redistribute unused samples.
    - Use sigma(sample)*dsMetres for optical depth and the existing analytic constant-segment
      scattering integral. Apply native depth-bin weights at each integration step; preserve all
      six OIT outputs without flattening the whole cloud to a representative transparency depth.
    - Native columns define a discontinuous density field. Thin features may fall between finite
      samples; quantify pointwise, temporal and silhouette errors against independent references.
      Retain exact uniform-density optical-depth fixtures. Exact integration of arbitrary columns
      belongs to the analytic oracle, not an additional production column-integration pass.

  Secondary budget and distance
    - Initial candidate LightRaySampleMax=10, supported 2..64, and ShadowDistance=1500 world units,
      supported 50..10000. Nonfinite distance restores 1500. Clip to the finite map; never use main-
      view depth or visibility to reject off-camera shadow density.
    - Anchor the schedule at the shading point. For D=ShadowDistance and N=LightRaySampleMax,
      use interval boundaries b_i=D*(i/N)^2, i=0..N, positions s_i=(b_i+b_(i+1))/2 and lengths
      d_i=b_(i+1)-b_i. Accumulate tau+=sigma(origin+direction*s_i)*d_i*metresPerWorldUnit.
      The sample is the arithmetic midpoint of quadratic boundaries, not D*((i+.5)/N)^2.
    - Use deterministic midpoint positions for solar and lunar shadows; no PMJ, blue-noise jitter,
      optical-depth weighting or per-column budget reset enters the shadow schedule. Reject proven
      zero density before detailed evaluation, but leave all remaining scheduled positions unchanged.
    - Positions outside the finite supported domain contribute zero. Stop when the ray has exited
      that domain with no possible reentry; do not rescale D or redistribute positions after such
      an exit. Respect the complete quadratic schedule for rays that remain inside supported bounds.
      An existing certified vertical bound may similarly prove remaining samples empty.
    - The same density definition is evaluated independently at every scheduled primary/shadow
      position. No replacement vertical profile, density volume or invented border extension is added.
    - At selected default budgets after bounded secondary integration, a primary ray has at most
      768 secondary rays and 7680 secondary full-detail evaluations with both sources active, plus
      at most 384 primary evaluations; these are policy
      upper bounds, not baseline observations. Report actual early exits and skipped zero sources.
    - Optional diagnostics compare 1500,3000 and complete supported-domain shadow distances with
      other settings fixed. Report distance bias separately from sampling bias; neither is an
      automated quality gate or a requirement to increase sample count/distance.

  Sampling and temporal identity
    - Use spatial blue-noise primary-ray offsets with an eight-frame temporal index advancing every
      rendered frame, independent of reconstruction phase. Use the traced full-view coordinate;
      a hash or PMJ point sequence alone is not a spatial blue-noise mask. Use existing resource
      ownership/binding APIs for any required noise asset; do not import an external engine runtime.
    - Separate jitter evolution from spatial reconstruction scheduling. Quarter-dimension trace to
      half-dimension reconstruction has a 2x2/four-frame phase cycle in reconstruction coordinates.
      Full-view upsampling does not turn that into sixteen phases. The retained Half compatibility
      mode may keep its existing phase policy until its own reconstruction adaptation is verified.
    - Shadow midpoints have no temporal jitter. The deterministic full-view reference uses primary
      midpoint steps and the same quadratic shadow midpoints. Release the cloud PMJ resource/bindings
      when no retained legacy comparison consumer uses them; leave unrelated consumers untouched.
    - Reject history on any change to sample budgets/distances, lattice/jitter policy, termination,
      trace/reconstruction layout or atmosphere application policy, as well as existing lifecycle,
      camera, medium, calibration and completed lighting incompatibilities.

  Termination policy
    - Candidate view transmittance threshold=.005; supported range [0,.005], with zero disabling this
      optimization. It controls the primary exit directly; its value alone does not qualify OIT quality.
    - Break the primary march after the current integration step if overall transmittance is below
      the threshold. Do not add a remaining-support prepass, a second attenuation/bin march or a
      residual-error subsystem. Retain ordinary completed-lighting admission and finite storage bounds.
    - Verify configured termination at the correct sample boundary and retain finite arithmetic.
      Record differences in revealage, bins, accumulators, glow and metadata without automated
      approximation-quality limits. Threshold selection uses measured cost and user appearance review.
    - Shadow rays initially evaluate the bounded quadratic schedule until finite-domain exit, with
      no optical-depth saturation shortcut. Keep tau finite and handle zero octave occlusion without
      zero*infinity. Additional shortcuts are optional future work requiring separate evidence.

  Quality selections and clean-break settings
    - Introduce separately named ViewRaySampleMax, ViewRaySampleMin,
      ViewBudgetDistance, LightRaySampleMax, ShadowDistance, StopTransmittance and TraceScale fields
      through the existing configuration owner. Keep detail fields and FullResolutionReference.
    - TraceScale is an enum with Half and Quarter; initial compatibility selection is Half. Quarter
      becomes the normal selection only after correctness, performance and user appearance review.
      FullResolutionReference overrides trace scale, disables temporal history and uses midpoint
      primary sampling and quadratic shadow midpoints; it is not the independent high-budget oracle.
    - User direction requires a clean break: no migration, compatibility converter, schema-version
      metadata or future-version preservation subsystem. Missing current fields use current defaults;
      obsolete fields are ignored, never converted into new counts. Preserve unrelated settings through
      the existing owner and save normally, not per frame. Retire replaced source-level fields too.
    - Validate absent Clouds, empty Clouds, obsolete ViewSamples, explicit new budgets, invalid bounds,
      nonfinite distances/thresholds and partial current-setting updates. Primary defaults are
      384/2/50; light controls are 10/1500 and StopTransmittance is .005. Layout controls receive
      their stated defaults when implemented.

  Correctness verification and optional approximation diagnostics
    - Keep independent scalar/native-box oracles for the specified sample lattice, shared density,
      finite domain, units and OIT/encoding formulas. Assert equivalent implementations on the same
      scheduled positions, with tolerances appropriate to floating-point arithmetic.
    - Keep exact uniform-density Beer-Lambert checks only when density is uniform over the complete
      sampled span and early termination is disabled or does not trigger. Keep settings/budget bounds,
      deterministic replay, source combinations, octave endpoints, finite outputs, depth clipping,
      resource lifetimes and history-rejection correctness.
    - Native support is discontinuous. Production versus exact/high-budget integration, reference
      doubling, approximation changes, image envelopes, temporal means and filter RMS/noise comparisons
      are optional measurements without approximation-quality acceptance assertions. They do not block
      implementation completion or mandate increased sample counts, tracing distances or resolution.
    - Diagnostic references must state their own approximation/stability limitations. Report physical
      RGB and OIT differences with explicit normalization and coverage; a normalized radiometric error
      is not a perceptual error. No mandatory reference-convergence or approximation-error threshold
      applies, including the former .01/.02/.021/.045 limits or frozen-baseline RMS requirements.
    - Finite attenuation bounds and admitted foreground clipping remain correctness requirements for
      every frame. Contributing metadata describes the steps that actually contributed, clipped to
      the admitted span; it need not match exact unsampled support. Authored temporal arithmetic and
      stale-history rejection remain tested independently of denoising effectiveness.
    - Approximation assertions and unused dense-reference machinery are removed in Phase 3.1.
      Current correctness verification needs no approximation-test exclusions. Neither a passing
      correctness suite nor diagnostics establish appearance acceptance or measured performance.

  Requirement reconciliation
    - Primary per-column multiplication -> Phase 2; secondary multiplication/distance -> Phase 3;
      strict/late termination -> Phase 4; screen-position count -> Phase 5; repeated atmosphere ->
      Phase 6; repeated tree descent/clock replay -> Phase 7; repeated density/phase work -> Phase 6.
    - Proposal half-dimension default is the legacy/current contract; Phase 5 may replace it only
      after the declared lower-resolution gates. Preserved per-column sample counts, PMJ identity and
      exact arbitrary-column attenuation govern legacy/reference comparisons. The future direct march
      replaces those constraints with fixed distance steps, primary blue noise and shadow midpoints.
    - With termination inactive, complete-span uniform-density attenuation remains exact within numeric
      tolerance. Discontinuous/detailed
      quadrature differences are diagnostic; acceleration equivalence is measured with the new lattice held
      fixed. No requirement to use a tree remains if measured cost favors cheap rejection or direct marching.
    - PBR.Clouds.md remains current-state architecture until runtime changes are verified. Preserve
      atmospheric publication, native producer/lifetimes, finite density bounds, six-target OIT,
      separate scene/material/particle/liquid depth and the unresolved reflection dependency.

