    compatible history. All optical factors share weights; projected support and current destination
    depth accompany them. Missing support yields neutral OIT, never a hidden transport march.
    Resolve forms each tap's native products before interpolation and encodes RGB once at submission.
  Lifetime/storage -> archived hierarchy ownership and parent lifecycle requirements -> stable
    allocations/attachments persist; only successful OIT commit advances history roles. Layout
    changes/reset retire both owned arrays while borrowed native/depth inputs survive. Combined
    nominal bytes are 48*tracePixels+96*historyPixels for Quarter, 144*historyPixels for Half,
    bounded to 1 GiB before creation; this is not measured resident memory.
  Correctness -> current correctness-only policy and Phase 5 verification -> four focused GPU tests
    cover actual odd-edge phase/depth identities, independently authored shared weights and history
    blending, all three foreground clips, and once-only encoding. Existing history and OIT tests
    retain their distinct contracts. Production scheduler checks cover reuse, commit/rejection,
